nswer/id #uuid "ec6a8eb8-c109-597e-96bb-9b561c34d9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391410} {:answer/id #uuid "f80fe0e9-1b8c-541a-b5cc-2a5606205e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391410} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d21f3c6-f7fb-5b6c-8dd7-e35248a7f05f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391409} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4e740e-764f-5d22-86c6-d4dcab01dfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391413} {:answer/id #uuid "a672958d-b9fd-537e-8f60-dcc5a9372518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391414} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a717c-b027-5816-86e7-e13b003665f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391415} {:answer/id #uuid "6a776299-946a-5a02-82fc-feb758ea43db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10{x}^{2}-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391415} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8d7bd14-76a5-5b01-9389-7f463996bec4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391412} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc71ef21-ab95-5a98-aab6-b8c717356264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391418} {:answer/id #uuid "38e92c9e-489f-5561-86aa-21233c9a65be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391418} :answer-field/id #uuid "c322ded2-adc3-590e-9217-7eb66344715b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "64f4cde4-f8ab-5629-93ea-f22c06ec9430" :question/math-academy-id "q-334108" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}-2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=10x-5{x}^{3},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}}, which is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ ({x}^{3}-2x)^{′} & (10x-5{x}^{3})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2 & 10-15{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-2x)(10-15{x}^{2})-(10x-5{x}^{3})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot 5(2-3{x}^{2})-5x(2-{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2})-5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x-(-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b182f484-befa-50e8-80e3-c6f147624f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391422} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad16653-3659-5bd7-a74c-cc62a1cf1c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391423} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ef68bc-40c6-565a-8e8d-f4095e0889f2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "713dc9ce-04fd-5fa5-bfe7-8abc8ccbbcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391425} {:answer/id #uuid "a56e147b-eda5-5939-8e23-ca63cfb432d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391426} :answer-field/id #uuid "d913da5f-b12a-58c7-b80e-1bbe27ab9c85" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f11b2387-2fc9-542d-8ba8-2598d7c3d41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391428} {:answer/id #uuid "de129c6c-ce47-5263-a095-78981544a286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391429} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e7fb56-bdf0-522f-9aaa-ecde39217ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391430} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b63844-1540-53e8-8fdf-1d085e57bd56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391430} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8be5cb-781b-53f4-bd01-09212a004cc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0932b3bc-4ddc-5e49-8937-50f983b26a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-334114" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′} & {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x-({cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d0d86d-c06f-5faf-a0ed-e8ee4d8b2010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8a/8ac5a8137dd22e9f7333315bc2f5e1120cdb46dc6fb84f938890c491563a86c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393935} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb52152-df73-5351-900a-b8e3c2fe2eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f5/f59924b67013ab56263c9e58d283bd29b1869699488702fffc8f88d1de18e647.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393936} {:answer/id #uuid "d01da734-6649-516c-965d-904f6eb30e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/41/4110727f4203228259f1ee4d95593e54c92731e986eb3bdcf9cc0692cf902aa4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393937} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ccb688-6ce7-56f2-8a91-5c430e62080a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/be/bec2fa9f6c2673fe9bc71fbdadb8f2cbf4c37ef05a38dd2486be3fac7e2b98d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393938} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c43a80-cb88-5719-a3f8-6293badb40f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/75/757072d5f7b2edaf1eb22e3e1a1c4135a387293396c9a4213ee3a2e213102ee6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393939} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6588a4-c656-5e02-9be8-4235169132b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9720bec0-3da4-5217-8468-e4569ac0e63c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79563" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =3x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/64/64b0962fbb38b2c64da68de01d1c6407174aa66f810b820ece7185866b7f8ff3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "893f4780-2c8b-522b-8087-2fc24822ebd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395764} {:answer/id #uuid "93cd5cdd-b047-544f-b7e7-fee685cf62f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "95\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395765} {:answer/id #uuid "ef24ffa0-3d6e-5335-9009-9906ff059c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395766} {:answer/id #uuid "d38f97ca-3e74-5b74-90a6-ef1513732aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "89\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395767} {:answer/id #uuid "96517c6b-bae9-503f-9f98-d3f004b861a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "67\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395765} :answer-field/id #uuid "fca47f9c-32af-5d7e-92a5-e9a1ab61bc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02ce9872-8986-5e67-bea1-b49d589c24ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-15589" :question/problem "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${25}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=90,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =25+75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 65 & =75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{13}{15} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{13}{15}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{13}{15}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the water has a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 25+75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the temperature of water will be ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{13}{15}\\right)}≈95\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b512b5-d109-5b7f-afe6-8012cddf53df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=12\\,510-18P,\\,P(0)=690" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395854} {:answer/id #uuid "451e0e4f-f1c3-5f2c-a984-6761ce64e2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.5P-1\\,23,\\,P(0)=2\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395855} {:answer/id #uuid "30e14fdd-8b43-5b70-9434-f7996dbad376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=9P-6\\,066,\\,P(0)=680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395856} {:answer/id #uuid "c372f938-bf0a-5da5-a0e8-947b58733787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,580-0.6P,\\,P(0)=4\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395857} {:answer/id #uuid "848a4d3c-7246-5b73-b528-cdd85bf23fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=16\\,256-4P,\\,P(0)=4\\,070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395854} :answer-field/id #uuid "f34c38b1-0c67-5df9-bd25-f72ef39097ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6960a095-e760-5362-9086-48e7e2e653fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-37180" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=12\\,510-18P,\\,P(0)=690,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=12\\,510-18P=18(695-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=18,a=695,$ and ${y}_{0}=690.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=16\\,256-4P=4(4\\,064-P),\\,P(0)=4\\,070,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,580-0.6P=0.6(4\\,300-P),\\,P(0)=4\\,400\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=4\\,070>a=4\\,064,$ and ${y}_{0}=4\\,400>a=4\\,300.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2368068-c473-5919-aff6-53a14b246b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396122} {:answer/id #uuid "801b88de-1112-5c33-bd1b-ddb864de2c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396123} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd49079-798b-58f7-8490-cd6fb613a033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396124} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b72ba2-9df1-5acc-b385-173c4e36f9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$18$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396125} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef4ba21-7e9c-5949-8c09-0a45d37f8298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "480a616c-8c80-5f81-b3d5-aa7488c0665b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7596" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=18.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=18,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=3\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}=6\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcb6c4d2-fc5f-560f-9373-4f7a33529527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396129} {:answer/id #uuid "6565345d-7cca-5723-8a4a-06b781ec17a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396130} {:answer/id #uuid "71576bd8-08e5-58e9-999c-df3860cd2885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ and $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396131} {:answer/id #uuid "b48bdeab-9afb-508b-a31f-840d2716535b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396132} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd4bf3f-6eb6-53a8-9729-d9955e874477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396129} :answer-field/id #uuid "abca09ba-0b0a-5885-90fb-fd1d7d5af56c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e572cb8d-0721-59ec-897a-5e0fa685feb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-7597" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=20$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=20,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "530d441f-02a7-58b9-ab67-967bf0eb2b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=28{y}^{2}-840" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397845} {:answer/id #uuid "129b2ea8-1b76-5424-95c9-8017f737962f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840y-28{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397846} {:answer/id #uuid "0659f1ed-771e-5b24-b08f-cc16c97621ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397847} {:answer/id #uuid "84008fcd-ee7c-5c1d-b05c-17d7861ef713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840+28y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397848} {:answer/id #uuid "65b83ad8-4ba9-5abe-ac5c-3f8478868400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397847} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b25d0f3-fc25-51e5-b7b1-1eea7cc7a67a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f61f141-3dc0-53a6-8909-845a088a0525" :question/math-academy-id "q-106654" :question/problem "Given that $y>30$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-28(y-30).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=28,$ and $a=30.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>30$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12647afe-fb4f-5f2b-9326-19bf07c40a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397886} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd2a414-b8e6-52ba-9a39-7d6bbb93cce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397887} {:answer/id #uuid "6612145e-ab64-58c5-8ac9-574cb5ec22a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397888} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed38fbd-9ab4-5313-a9e7-428d7d2e672f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e45ac-2ae6-5e93-b31c-67cedb5a0fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad59f961-3401-5e50-8b21-92d3facaf654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fee906ee-def7-5194-a87e-a1f77108aa56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107125" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${60}^{∘}C.$ After $10$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${52}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${40}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${28}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={28}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${60}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=60,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 60 & =28+K \\\\ K & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+32{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${52}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(10)=52,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =28+32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ 24 & =32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =-10k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${40}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =40 \\\\ 28+32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =40 \\\\ 32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =12 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${40}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}≈34\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2339eddd-c520-53fc-835c-406bf5642b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}+C{x}^{2}+Dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398202} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfc929f-0e67-5e47-80b6-d2949d1be4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{4}+C{x}^{2}+Dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398203} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd5ffc9-d74f-592b-a383-53ba3803f136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398204} {:answer/id #uuid "395e4984-cf1d-534b-b01f-09b03aa00ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{5}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398205} {:answer/id #uuid "796c5808-234b-5385-a5e5-513d3b55563c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{4}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398204} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7d8f65-a93b-5daa-b8fa-34a0b4f4a5ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f503fbb-9abb-5178-8aa2-5b5187c150ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-119039" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=20{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =20\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+C \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5{x}^{4}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+Cx+D \\\\  & ={x}^{5}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}]}
